WEBVTT


00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
Downloaded @ subs4free.club


00:00:04.107 --> 00:00:06.527
<i>The Shirley Temple program
usually seen at this time</i>


00:00:06.652 --> 00:00:07.778
<i>will not be presented</i>


00:00:07.945 --> 00:00:10.490
<i>in order that we may bring
you the following special broadcast.</i>


00:00:11.115 --> 00:00:12.824
<i>We have interrupted this program</i>


00:00:12.951 --> 00:00:15.327
<i>for a special broadcast
from Cape Canaveral.</i>


00:00:16.036 --> 00:00:21.042
<i>May 5th, 1961, certainly a day
to be entered in the history books.</i>


00:00:21.667 --> 00:00:25.338
<i>Although the astronaut launch
is being seen close up</i>


00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.507
<i>and live on television screens,</i>


00:00:28.048 --> 00:00:31.844
<i>hundreds of persons traveled
to Cocoa Beach to see with their own eyes</i>


00:00:31.928 --> 00:00:36.473
<i>at long distance, the streak of flame
as the Redstone heads skyward.</i>


00:00:44.064 --> 00:00:46.484
<i>I don't know any words
for this except the trite ones.</i>


00:00:46.776 --> 00:00:49.070
<i>Tension is mounting here
at Cape Canaveral.</i>


00:00:50.195 --> 00:00:52.281
<i>The world watches with interest.</i>


00:00:52.573 --> 00:00:56.828
<i>The public has no trouble feeling concern
for the man who lies on his back</i>


00:00:57.119 --> 00:00:59.204
<i>at the nose end of a Redstone missile.</i>


00:00:59.496 --> 00:01:02.875
<i>Somehow, this doesn't seem to be the place
for a human being.</i>


00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:09.173
<i>We're looking at this as
eventually a big exploration.</i>


00:01:09.424 --> 00:01:11.174
<i>We hope to lay the foundation</i>


00:01:11.509 --> 00:01:14.429
<i>for much broader exploration
in the future.</i>


00:01:16.847 --> 00:01:19.349
<i>I'm sure you've given some thought
to the possibility that</i>


00:01:19.433 --> 00:01:22.103
<i>this flight may not turn out well
and that you may not come back.</i>


00:01:25.772 --> 00:01:28.567
<i>One man, backed up
by a team of 10,000,</i>


00:01:28.942 --> 00:01:32.280
<i>backed up by the most elaborate devices
that science can invent.</i>


00:01:35.158 --> 00:01:39.954
<i>But still, one man, alone, in a tiny shell
on top of a rocket.</i>


00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:54.718
When we asked NASA for some pictures
of the original American astronauts,


00:01:54.843 --> 00:01:58.139
John Glenn and the six others chosen
in 1959 for Project Mercury,


00:01:58.222 --> 00:01:59.222
look what we got.


00:02:00.474 --> 00:02:03.019
<i>The Seven in their spacesuits
as formal as a class picture</i>


00:02:03.102 --> 00:02:04.436
<i>or a management training group.</i>


00:02:05.103 --> 00:02:08.231
<i>But these, too, are the men
who are going to fly off into space</i>


00:02:08.316 --> 00:02:10.943
<i>and catch us up with the Russians
and win the Cold War</i>


00:02:11.026 --> 00:02:14.155
<i>against Sputnik with their hair down
and their burnouses up</i>


00:02:14.237 --> 00:02:16.740
<i>after four days of survival training
in the desert.</i>


00:02:20.411 --> 00:02:21.454
<i>What were the astronauts?</i>


00:02:22.454 --> 00:02:26.709
<i>Tall in-the-capsule superheroes
or just a bunch of regular fighter jocks?</i>


00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.300
<i>Well, writer Tom Wolfe has spent a number
of years now looking into their story</i>


00:02:34.384 --> 00:02:37.260
<i>and he has written it into a book called,
The Right Stuff.</i>


00:02:39.013 --> 00:02:42.013
<i>It's not the kind of story that we heard
at the time from the space managers</i>


00:02:42.057 --> 00:02:44.686
<i>and from the politicians,
or even from the American press.</i>


00:02:46.978 --> 00:02:48.665
Tom Wolfe, nice to have you
with us this morning.


00:02:48.689 --> 00:02:51.192
- Tom, good to see you.
- Uh, were we in need of heroes


00:02:51.275 --> 00:02:52.651
when these astronauts came along?


00:02:52.735 --> 00:02:56.698
Is that why we were so eager
to build them up into kind of false gods?


00:02:56.989 --> 00:03:01.661
Right now, it's so hard to remember
what a... how seriously the Cold War


00:03:01.743 --> 00:03:04.122
was taken back in the... in the late '50s.


00:03:24.307 --> 00:03:27.478
<i>You are the first Americans
to see this launching of Sputnik 1</i>


00:03:28.146 --> 00:03:31.399
<i>from the desert of Kyzylkum,
in the Soviet Union.</i>


00:03:34.317 --> 00:03:37.195
And now back tonight and trying
for 20,000 dollars are Eddie Hodges,


00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:40.283
the ten-year-old schoolboy,
and his partner, Major John Glenn, Jr.,


00:03:40.365 --> 00:03:41.616
the Marine Corps jet pilot.


00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.120
Uh, what do you think
of the Russian satellite,


00:03:44.203 --> 00:03:47.706
which is circling the earth
at 18,000 miles per hour?


00:03:48.123 --> 00:03:51.377
It's the first time anybody has ever been
able to get anything out that far


00:03:51.461 --> 00:03:54.004
in space and keep it there
for any length of time.


00:03:54.171 --> 00:03:58.675
And this is probably the first step
toward space travel or moon travel,


00:03:58.759 --> 00:04:01.612
something we'll probably run into
maybe in Eddie's lifetime here at least.


00:04:01.637 --> 00:04:04.223
Eddie, would you like
to take a trip to the moon?


00:04:04.306 --> 00:04:06.057
No, sir, I like it fine right here.


00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:18.612
<i>Democrat Senator Jackson
of Washington describes</i>


00:04:18.696 --> 00:04:22.867
<i>the Russian achievement as a devastating
blow to the prestige of The United States.</i>


00:04:23.158 --> 00:04:26.620
<i>As the satellite travels around the world
once every hour and a half,</i>


00:04:26.788 --> 00:04:29.540
<i>its radio transmitters,
powered by chemical batteries,</i>


00:04:29.665 --> 00:04:32.834
<i>that are apparently sending back
coded messages to the Russians.</i>


00:04:36.338 --> 00:04:38.591
<i>The White House sizes
up the situation this way,</i>


00:04:38.673 --> 00:04:40.514
<i>The launching of Russia's
and the world's first</i>


00:04:40.675 --> 00:04:45.514
<i>artificial moon is of great scientific
interest but comes as no surprise.</i>


00:04:46.098 --> 00:04:48.518
<i>General, are you awed
by the Russian accomplishment</i>


00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:49.601
<i>with this big Sputnik?</i>


00:04:49.685 --> 00:04:52.146
<i>You're only awed
by the things that you don't understand</i>


00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:53.855
<i>or don't believe someone can do.</i>


00:04:54.273 --> 00:04:56.858
In other words, we know what
they had to know to do this?


00:04:57.317 --> 00:04:59.528
- Certainly, we know it.
- Why haven't we done it?


00:04:59.987 --> 00:05:01.363
Well, we got started late.


00:05:01.697 --> 00:05:04.074
We didn't get about the job
as early as we might have.


00:05:04.408 --> 00:05:06.661
Now we have to work
like blazes to catch up.


00:05:09.413 --> 00:05:11.958
It means they're getting ahead of us
and we certainly need to...


00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:13.668
start working hard to catch up.


00:05:16.545 --> 00:05:19.464
I think it's about time America woke up
and did something about it.


00:05:21.216 --> 00:05:25.178
<i>There was a sense in this country
that it was all important...</i>


00:05:25.303 --> 00:05:27.305
<i>to catch up with the Russians in space.</i>


00:05:27.639 --> 00:05:30.451
<i>John McCormick, who was then the Speaker
of the House of Representatives,</i>


00:05:30.475 --> 00:05:32.555
<i>was getting up and saying
we face national extinction</i>


00:05:32.603 --> 00:05:34.312
<i>if we do not catch up with the Russians.</i>


00:05:44.197 --> 00:05:48.076
<i>In desperation, the United States
looked to the Vanguard.</i>


00:05:51.247 --> 00:05:54.292
<i>Nearly 200 newsmen
from all over the world were flown down</i>


00:05:54.375 --> 00:05:55.459
<i>for the big turkey shoot.</i>


00:05:57.211 --> 00:05:59.797
<i>At the launching site, they were given
a play-by-play account.</i>


00:06:00.505 --> 00:06:04.259
<i>They witnessed each tiny detail
of the usually top-secret preparation.</i>


00:06:05.136 --> 00:06:08.848
<i>And inside the block house,
the tension steadily mounted.</i>


00:06:28.658 --> 00:06:32.997
<i>America's prestige had
never been lower than at this moment,</i>


00:06:33.163 --> 00:06:38.586
<i>11:45 a.m., December 6, 1957.</i>


00:06:44.466 --> 00:06:50.514
There is a tremendous gap
between promise and performance.


00:06:51.432 --> 00:06:54.476
<i>I believe the American people
want action...</i>


00:06:58.439 --> 00:07:01.651
<i>and are demanding that
we get going with our program.</i>


00:07:10.076 --> 00:07:12.875
Public opinion in the civilized world
has grown accustomed to


00:07:12.951 --> 00:07:14.788
fast scientific progress.


00:07:14.913 --> 00:07:18.875
<i>Already, the idea of Sputnik whirling
through space has become accepted</i>


00:07:18.959 --> 00:07:23.172
<i>and people are saying, "What comes next?
What comes after Sputnik? After Vanguard?"</i>


00:07:23.588 --> 00:07:28.427
Well, the next step has been planned
for a long time. It is a manned satellite.


00:07:40.355 --> 00:07:43.358
<i>Catch up on all fronts.
That was the imperative.</i>


00:07:43.776 --> 00:07:47.988
<i>So a so-called quick and dirty approach
was seized upon.</i>


00:07:48.906 --> 00:07:51.283
<i>They would try to launch not a flying ship</i>


00:07:51.617 --> 00:07:56.329
<i>but a pod, a container,
a capsule... with a man in it.</i>


00:07:58.624 --> 00:08:00.125
<i>The man would not be a pilot.</i>


00:08:01.084 --> 00:08:02.752
<i>He would be a human cannonball.</i>


00:08:03.629 --> 00:08:07.298
<i>He would not be able to alter the course
of the capsule in the slightest.</i>


00:08:08.341 --> 00:08:10.845
<i>The job was assigned to NACA,</i>


00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:13.848
<i>the National Advisory Committee
for Aeronautics,</i>


00:08:15.016 --> 00:08:17.393
<i>which was converted into NASA.</i>


00:08:21.187 --> 00:08:23.815
We will be developing and launching
into space,


00:08:24.399 --> 00:08:29.196
vehicles needed to obtain scientific data
and to explore the solar system.


00:08:31.115 --> 00:08:33.783
<i>N.A.S.A. will have
about 300 million dollars</i>


00:08:33.908 --> 00:08:36.578
<i>for its program in fiscal 1959,</i>


00:08:37.413 --> 00:08:40.165
<i>and with this appropriation,
we must press forward</i>


00:08:40.331 --> 00:08:42.375
<i>the current research programs
in our laboratories.</i>


00:08:43.418 --> 00:08:48.090
<i>We must contract for work by others
in such fields as electronics and guidance</i>


00:08:48.506 --> 00:08:51.342
<i>and other areas where we have
neither the special competence,</i>


00:08:52.219 --> 00:08:53.721
<i>nor the facilities that are needed.</i>


00:08:54.554 --> 00:08:56.765
<i>We must accelerate
our development programs.</i>


00:08:58.768 --> 00:09:02.437
<i>We must acquire the vehicles that
will carry our data-gathering apparatus,</i>


00:09:02.897 --> 00:09:05.732
<i>and ultimately man, into space.</i>


00:09:15.658 --> 00:09:18.245
<i>The program to pick the
first man to American to try to pilot</i>


00:09:18.328 --> 00:09:20.538
<i>a rocket into orbit in space has begun.</i>


00:09:20.873 --> 00:09:23.542
<i>U.S. space chief T. Keith Glennan
announced tonight</i>


00:09:23.625 --> 00:09:27.087
<i>the American manned satellite program
will be called Project Mercury,</i>


00:09:27.837 --> 00:09:30.298
<i>its pilots will be known as
Mercury Aeronauts,</i>


00:09:30.381 --> 00:09:32.092
<i>and one of their number, all volunteers,</i>


00:09:32.217 --> 00:09:33.594
<i>will make the first spaceflight.</i>


00:09:35.303 --> 00:09:38.144
<i>Two months before the astronauts
were chosen they were still considering</i>


00:09:38.230 --> 00:09:41.525
<i>using racing car drivers,
mountain climbers, scuba divers,</i>


00:09:41.976 --> 00:09:45.980
<i>infantry men, anybody who had faced
stress and dangerous situations</i>


00:09:46.065 --> 00:09:48.609
<i>successfully would be allowed
to apply for astronaut</i>


00:09:48.734 --> 00:09:50.413
<i>because there wasn't any flying
to be done.</i>


00:09:52.779 --> 00:09:56.116
<i>Finally, Eisenhower decided,
"Well, hell, we've got 500 test pilots</i>


00:09:56.241 --> 00:09:59.161
<i>"in the military. We can call them
to Washington tomorrow."</i>


00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:03.471
<i>So let's get them from this
group of people we can totally control</i>


00:10:03.499 --> 00:10:04.625
<i>and get on with it.</i>


00:10:05.835 --> 00:10:07.418
<i>So they bring in these test pilots.</i>


00:10:11.423 --> 00:10:14.592
<i>From all of the active duty pilots
in the Navy, Marines, and Air Force,</i>


00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:19.389
<i>the service records of 473 test pilots
were selected for review.</i>


00:10:20.599 --> 00:10:23.184
<i>110 met the basic qualifications.</i>


00:10:25.062 --> 00:10:29.984
<i>The Right Stuff is both
a code of behavior and a mystical belief.</i>


00:10:31.067 --> 00:10:35.239
<i>As test pilots, you have to be willing
to go up and hang your mortal hide out</i>


00:10:35.322 --> 00:10:39.033
<i>over the edge and then have the experience
and the moxie and the talent</i>


00:10:39.118 --> 00:10:41.327
<i>to pull it back in,
and then go up the next day</i>


00:10:41.412 --> 00:10:43.431
<i>and the next day and the next day
and the next day, even,</i>


00:10:43.455 --> 00:10:45.165
<i>uh, the series is infinite.</i>


00:11:00.806 --> 00:11:03.725
<i>Phase two of the selection program</i>


00:11:04.058 --> 00:11:07.437
<i>was a very thorough physical examination.</i>


00:11:08.647 --> 00:11:11.774
<i>And the men continued on
to the third phase.</i>


00:11:12.192 --> 00:11:17.155
<i>This phase involved exposure
to the acceleration, lowered pressure,</i>


00:11:17.364 --> 00:11:21.619
<i>noise, and other stresses expected
in space flight.</i>


00:11:26.248 --> 00:11:29.585
<i>At a Washington news conference,
officials introduced seven carefully</i>


00:11:29.710 --> 00:11:33.838
<i>chosen military test pilots
as America's first spacemen.</i>


00:11:38.384 --> 00:11:40.221
<i>How are the kids' appetites tonight?</i>


00:11:40.721 --> 00:11:42.264
Probably pretty good.


00:11:43.224 --> 00:11:45.225
<i>Better than usual
or worse than usual?</i>


00:11:45.433 --> 00:11:48.645
Well, probably a little bit, uh, they'll,
uh, be too excited.


00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:51.190
Maybe they won't eat
as much as they usually do.


00:11:52.066 --> 00:11:55.277
<i>Well, this is supposed
to have been a very tightly kept secret.</i>


00:11:55.402 --> 00:11:57.779
<i>How... how did you begin
to suspect something?</i>


00:11:58.364 --> 00:12:00.157
Well, listening to the news.


00:12:00.823 --> 00:12:04.119
<i>Well, all seven of the men
are officers and test pilots.</i>


00:12:04.202 --> 00:12:07.914
<i>Three Air Force, three Navy,
and one Marine. All are volunteers.</i>


00:12:08.289 --> 00:12:10.960
<i>Doctor T. Keith Glennan,
National Aeronautical</i>


00:12:11.043 --> 00:12:15.172
<i>and Space administrator introduced
them this afternoon at a news conference.</i>


00:12:15.755 --> 00:12:20.844
Which of these men will be first
to orbit the Earth, I cannot tell you.


00:12:21.803 --> 00:12:24.514
He won't know himself
until the day of the flight.


00:12:26.015 --> 00:12:28.601
<i>It's my pleasure to introduce... to you,</i>


00:12:28.894 --> 00:12:31.312
<i>and I consider it a very real honor,
gentlemen...</i>


00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:35.900
<i>from your right, Malcolm S. Carpenter,</i>


00:12:37.403 --> 00:12:41.782
Leroy G. Cooper, John H. Glenn,


00:12:42.825 --> 00:12:44.201
Virgil I. Grissom,


00:12:45.827 --> 00:12:47.413
<i>Walter M. Schirra,</i>


00:12:49.373 --> 00:12:54.294
Alan B. Shepard, Donald K. Slayton.


00:12:56.087 --> 00:13:00.801
These, ladies, and gentlemen,
are the nation's Mercury astronauts.


00:13:06.472 --> 00:13:10.351
<i>The question everybody wants to ask,
"What do the wives and children</i>


00:13:10.436 --> 00:13:13.355
<i>"of these men think of their ambitions
to go into space?"</i>


00:13:13.813 --> 00:13:16.317
My wife's attitude toward this
has been the same as it has been


00:13:16.399 --> 00:13:18.610
all along through all my flying,
that, uh...


00:13:18.902 --> 00:13:21.697
if it's what I want to do
and she's behind it


00:13:21.779 --> 00:13:23.282
and the kids are, too, a 100 percent.


00:13:25.075 --> 00:13:27.715
Well, my wife feels the same way or,
of course, I couldn't be here.


00:13:28.662 --> 00:13:32.582
And she's, uh, with me all the way
and the boys are too little to, uh,


00:13:33.042 --> 00:13:35.682
realize what's going on yet,
but I'm sure they'd feel the same way.


00:13:36.294 --> 00:13:40.215
My wife has agreed that professional
opinions are mine, career's mine,


00:13:40.716 --> 00:13:42.384
but we also have to have a family life


00:13:42.467 --> 00:13:44.470
that we like and this is part
of the agreement.


00:13:45.136 --> 00:13:47.806
I have no problems at home.
My family's in complete agreement.


00:13:54.020 --> 00:13:56.315
<i>John Glenn was actually
unique among the seven</i>


00:13:56.398 --> 00:13:57.649
<i>in terms of his personality.</i>


00:13:57.982 --> 00:14:00.652
<i>I'm John Glenn,
I'm the lonesome Marine on this outfit</i>


00:14:00.818 --> 00:14:04.947
and I'm, uh, 37. I, jokingly, uh,
of course, said that, uh,


00:14:05.032 --> 00:14:07.302
I got on this project
because it'd probably be the nearest


00:14:07.326 --> 00:14:09.912
to heaven I'd ever get, and I wanted
to make the most of it.


00:14:10.078 --> 00:14:11.538
But, uh...


00:14:11.788 --> 00:14:16.125
my feelings are that this whole project
with regard to... to space sort of stands


00:14:16.293 --> 00:14:18.961
with us now as... as if you wanna look
at it one way,


00:14:19.046 --> 00:14:21.756
like the Wright brothers stood
at Kitty Hawk about 50 years ago.


00:14:22.633 --> 00:14:24.884
<i>At the very first press conference
John Glenn proved</i>


00:14:24.967 --> 00:14:27.136
<i>to be the most articulate of the seven.</i>


00:14:27.221 --> 00:14:29.740
My wife made a remark the other day,
I've been out of this world


00:14:29.764 --> 00:14:31.725
for a long time I might as well
go on out there.


00:14:33.936 --> 00:14:35.312
Next question, please.


00:14:35.979 --> 00:14:39.191
<i>He had a kind of countrified
sophistication, if you will,</i>


00:14:39.524 --> 00:14:43.736
<i>and he had a great freckle-faced smile
and was just great at handling</i>


00:14:44.028 --> 00:14:45.614
<i>what we now call the media.</i>


00:14:46.322 --> 00:14:51.077
The question is, "Would the, uh,
gentlemen, uh, like to, uh...


00:14:51.912 --> 00:14:55.456
"say which, which test, uh,
they liked least?"


00:14:58.376 --> 00:15:00.671
Johnny Glenn, uh, you... you answer


00:15:00.754 --> 00:15:03.256
and then we'll start this way
and around that way.


00:15:03.966 --> 00:15:06.677
That's a real tough one
because we had some pretty good tests,


00:15:06.759 --> 00:15:10.721
but I... I think, uh, it's rather
difficult to pick one, because if the...


00:15:10.889 --> 00:15:13.307
if you figure how many openings
there are on the human body


00:15:13.392 --> 00:15:15.436
and how far you can go in any one of them.


00:15:18.647 --> 00:15:22.317
You gave it away.


00:15:22.734 --> 00:15:26.363
Now... Now, you answer which one would
be the toughest for you.


00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:35.538
<i>So, after this one man, Glenn,
who's so articulate starts</i>


00:15:35.621 --> 00:15:38.500
<i>saying all these things about God,
country, family, all the rest,</i>


00:15:38.625 --> 00:15:41.919
<i>immediately there's the picture
of seven astronauts</i>


00:15:42.004 --> 00:15:46.133
<i>as these sort of God-fearing,
small town family men.</i>


00:15:47.259 --> 00:15:48.735
<i>And the rest of them were stuck with it.</i>


00:15:48.759 --> 00:15:51.080
<i>They either had a choice
of raising their hands and saying,</i>


00:15:51.138 --> 00:15:53.073
